# The waterfall gorge's visitor leaflet, its times and rules were made up for this lesson.
lesson writing-a-leaflet v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=5 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A leaflet with bullet points"
  goal "Lay out a leaflet with a title, headings and bullet points that follow one pattern, so a reader in a hurry finds what they need."

  look {
    scene 36x14 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        listpad pad title="On the bridge, please:" items=["• hold the rope", "• walk one by one", "• keep dogs on leads"] count=0 width=16 ticks=false
        ropebridge rb planks=10 gaps=2
      }
    }
    say """
      Visitors read a leaflet standing on a path, so its layout does half the work. A title names
      it, headings name its parts, and a warning that must not be missed sits apart at the top.
      Bullet points list separate points in any order. After a lead-in ending in a colon, every
      bullet finishes the lead-in, starts the same way and is punctuated the same way.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.leaflet-device k=0
    }
    show writing.leaflet-device k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show writing.bullet-parallel k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show writing.bullet-consistent k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show writing.leaflet-device k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
    show writing.bullet-parallel k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
  }
  story {
    show writing.bullet-from-prose k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show writing.bullet-consistent k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
    show writing.bullet-from-prose k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.bullet-fix k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.leaflet-write k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "A lead-in with a colon, then bullets that all finish it, start alike and end alike."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that layout guides a reader: titles, headings, bullets and a set-apart warning each
    do a job, and a bullet list is only easy to read when every bullet follows one pattern. The way
    in chooses the device for a part of the gorge's leaflet; the core finds the bullet that does not
    finish its lead-in, the list punctuated the same way all through, and the list that turns a
    paragraph into bullets without losing or adding a point. The wrong idea to watch for is mixing
    patterns (a capital and full stop on one bullet, none on the next) and bullets that do not
    follow on from the lead-in (you will need: bring a drink). If the child is stuck, read the
    lead-in aloud before each bullet. Off the page, collect leaflets from a museum or a park and
    judge their bullet lists together. This lesson takes writing-59 (headings, grade three) and
    writing-50 (numbered steps) a step on. England's programme of study for years 5 and 6 asks for
    "using further organisational and presentational devices to structure text and to guide the
    reader [for example, headings, bullet points, underlining]", and its year 6 grammar appendix
    adds punctuating bullet points consistently (recalled).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a device is chosen once in the teacher's pen first, the devices are a title, bullets and a heading, the odd bullet is a command among commands, the consistent list is the one of short phrases, and the two-star has two bullets to mend."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the devices are a numbered list and a warning box, the odd bullets are a command among things and a phrase with no verb, the consistent list ends without full stops after please, the paragraph to turn into bullets is about swimming, and the two-star has three bullets to mend in a list of things."
  }
}
